t it wasn't terrifying you know  I'm wondering how you ended up doing the  scholarly work you you you currently do  did it was it born out of your  experience in Cuba  you know what led you to do the work  that you do it's combination of things  for one thing my father was immensely  interested in the past and he collected  antiques so in our household the past  was always more important than the  present I think that had a lot to do  with it but the turning point for me  came very early first inkling that I  wanted to be a historian was when I was  living in a foster home for juvenile  delinquents in Miami and the only place  where I could escape was a public  library I went there every evening and  spent two three hours there and  gravitated to the history section and  pretty soon I had gone through all of  the children's section and and was given  permission to check out some of the  adult history books and that's when I  can identify I got hooked of course at  that point I didn't think I wanted to be  a professor because I didn't even know  what a professor yeah so I can't trace  it exactly to that but that's a moment  where I can I can say with you know with  clarity that's when I got hooked on on  history in the past but you write  predominantly about religious history  correct yes so let's talk a little bit  about you know that the work you're  doing now I'm particularly interested in  the the levitation and the the shift in  - towards miracles right because I work  in the area that's the transition from  medieval to modern right and what what  what distinguishes modern from medieval  what is modern well a modern there's a  mind shift away from the miraculous it  but only at the upper levels of the  intelligentsia when you say modern what  you're where it let's say invention of  the printing press 1450 changes learning  completely  so from 1450 on there are individuals  who begin to back away from medieval  beliefs in miracles and you know  steadily becomes more and more  acceptable among the intelligentsia and  especially among Protestants to say that  miracles have not happened since  biblical times what I study is the messy  part I study the the not only the  continuation of medieval attitudes but  among Catholics the embrace of those  attitudes and the exaggeration of those  attitudes so a subtitle for my project  is a history of the impossible I look at  miracles that you know we in our day and  age deemed totally impossible such as  levitation or by location being in two  places at the same that that these are  miracles that are not healing miracles  over which there are always question  marks was it natural or was it a miracle  but you know if you have accounts and  hundreds of witnesses who say so-and-so  was ten feet off the ground then as a  historian you've got a problem with  something like that well I say that you  know we bring really big brackets to put  around the problem where we don't ask  the question did so-and-so really  levitate ten feet off the ground but the  question becomes what  can we make of these testimonies how do  you explain this how do you explain it  it's hard it's very hard you know  occasionally I struggle with this  because at one talk when I was going on  and on at detail in detail about history  of the impossible someone raised their  hand and said excuse me you're not  dealing with history the impossible  you're dealing with the history of the  ridiculous well that's the danger it's  very thin ice that you go out on when  you start putting these things strictly  in context and and saying that you can't  really go much deeper than the belief  that people were perhaps convinced they  saw this and you know the question mark  remains over the event but you know as a  cultural historian or as a social  historian one can approach these  miracles and try to figure out what they  tell us about the transition to  modernity and how complex it was and you  know it's not like the belief in t